%% DM26-0670

%% A new session takes over from older ones of its identity and instance, as built (2026-11-04, docs/architecture/cassini-broker.md,
%% "One session per identity"). A single-instance workload's certificates name no instance, so its pods supersede each other. A
%% multi-instance workload's pods are each a session, and only a pod restarted in place (the same UID, a new key) supersedes its own.
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant New as new client
    participant NL as new Listener
    participant SM as SessionManager
    participant OS as old SessionAgent
    participant OL as old Listener
    participant Old as old client
    participant OSub as old SubscriberAgents
    participant NSub as new SubscriberAgent

    Note over Old,OL: Usually a replaced pod, registered before the broker has seen the old connection die. It may still look connected.
    New->>NL: RegistrationRequest (no session to resume, or one it cannot)
    NL->>SM: RegistrationRequest(identity, instance)
    SM->>SM: a new session, and every older one for which<br/>session::supersedes((identity, instance), its own) holds is marked superseded:<br/>it can no longer be resumed
    opt it still has a connection
        SM->>OS: CloseConnection(except: the new client)
        OS->>OL: close it
        OL--xOld: the connection closes
    end
    SM->>OSub: SessionSuperseded, through the SubscriberManager
    OSub->>OSub: stop forwarding, and hold what arrives (HoldSubscription)
    OSub-->>SM: SessionHeld, once they all hold
    SM->>OS: drain: drained, not stopped, so that its CloseConnection goes first
    SM-->>NL: RegistrationResponse(Ok new id)
    NL-->>New: RegistrationResponse(Ok new id)

    New->>NL: SubscribeRequest(topic), decided at the Listener
    NL->>NSub: CreateSubscriber, through the session, the broker and the topic
    NSub->>SM: GetHeldSessions: parked until no takeover of this identity and instance is in progress
    SM-->>NSub: the sessions it may claim from
    NSub->>OSub: ClaimHeld, oldest first
    OSub-->>NSub: what each held on this topic
    NSub->>New: numbered anew and delivered, then acknowledged as usual

    Note over SM,OSub: What nobody claims is let go a retention window after the takeover (end_expired_sessions).

    opt the old instance is still running, and comes back asking for its session
        Old->>SM: RegistrationRequest(the superseded id), by a new connection
        SM-->>Old: a new session, which supersedes nothing
        Note over Old,SM: So two live instances do not take turns closing each other.<br/>A single-instance workload that runs two has a bug this stops from thrashing.
    end
